4.2 Human Physiology

Basal metabolic rate under Mars gravity (3.72 m s⁻²) is treated as an uncertain parameter. The central estimate uses the linear scaling of Harris‑Benedict by the gravity ratio, but we explicitly note that this is an unvalidated extrapolation; therefore the crew metabolic model includes a multiplicative uncertainty factor f_m \sim \mathcal{N}(1,0.05) applied to all rates. Oxygen consumption: \dot{m}_{O_2} = f_m\times0.836 kg day⁻¹; CO₂: \dot{m}_{CO_2} = f_m\times1.000 kg day⁻¹; water intake: \dot{m}_{H_2O,in} = f_m\times3.56 kg day⁻¹. Activity multipliers (sleep 0.85, EVA 2.8) further modulate.

4.3 Oxygen Generation

PEM electrolysis: 2\mathrm{H_2O} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H_2} + \mathrm{O_2}. Minimum work w_{\min}=\Delta G^\circ=237.13 kJ mol⁻¹ O₂. Practical specific energy w_{\text{PEM}} = w_{\min}/(\eta_{\text{cell}}\eta_{\text{sys}}) \approx 52.5 kWh kg⁻¹ O₂. MOXIE solid‑oxide electrolysis: 2\mathrm{CO_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{CO} + \mathrm{O_2}; Nernst potential E = 1.46 - (RT/4F)\ln(P_{O_2}/P_{CO_2}^2); specific energy 385 kWh kg⁻¹ (from flight data). Storage at 350 bar: Redlich‑Kwong EOS, tank mass from composite stress formula (SF=3.5).

4.4 Carbon Dioxide Management

Sabatier: \mathrm{CO_2}+4\mathrm{H_2} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{CH_4}+2\mathrm{H_2O}. Rate law:

r = \frac{k K_{CO_2} K_{H_2}^2 P_{CO_2} P_{H_2}^2}{(1+K_{CO_2}P_{CO_2}+K_{H_2}P_{H_2})^3},

with k = A e^{-E_a/RT}, E_a=100 kJ mol⁻¹ (Falbo et al.). At 300 °C, single‑pass conversion 0.95. CO₂ scrubber dynamics: dP_{CO_2}/dt = (RT/V M_{CO_2})\dot{m}_{\text{prod}} - (Q_{\text{scrub}}/V)P_{CO_2}.

4.5 Water Recovery

Phase 1 (ISS‑like): VCD (RR=0.85), RO (flux J_w = A(\Delta P-\Delta\pi)), overall WRR 0.92. Phase 2: adding forward osmosis raises WRR to 0.975. Phase 3: bioregenerative transpiration closure raises to 0.995. Brine crystallisation energy: Q = \dot{m}_{\text{brine}}c_p\Delta T + \dot{m}_{\text{salt}}\Delta H_{\text{cryst}}.

4.6 Food Production

Monod growth: \mu = \mu_{\max} \frac{C_{CO_2}}{K_s+C_{CO_2}}\frac{I}{K_I+I}, with K_s=200 ppm, K_I=200 μmol m⁻² s⁻¹. This is a pragmatic approximation; model‑form uncertainty is acknowledged but not quantified in this specification.

4.7 Power and Thermal

Solar PV: P = I_{\text{surf}}A\eta_{\text{cell}}(1+\beta(T-25))(1-\delta_d). Nuclear reactor: one‑group diffusion to obtain 10 kWe at 28% efficiency. Battery: SOC dynamics with Arrhenius fade. Thermal: \rho c_p V dT/dt = \dot{Q}_{\text{int}} - UA(T-T_{\text{out}}) - \dot{m}_{\text{leak}}c_p(T-T_{\text{out}}). Radiator derating for dust: \eta_{\text{rad}} = 1-0.15(\tau-1).

4.8 Radiation Shielding

Exponential attenuation D(x) = D_0 e^{-x/\lambda} + D_{\text{albedo}}; GCR surface dose 0.210 mGy day⁻¹ (MSL/RAD). Shielding requirement for SPE: x = (1/\mu)\ln(D_{\text{event}}/D_{\text{allow}}).

5. System Architecture and Integration Design

5.1 Hybrid Co‑Simulation Overview

The simulator is composed of continuous‑time FMUs (Functional Mock‑up Units, FMI 3.0) for physical processes and a discrete‑event engine (SimPy) for crew schedules and stochastic failures. A master algorithm orchestrates the exchange every \Delta T_{\text{macro}} = 300 s. The choice of 300 s is based on the stiffness analysis: it is shorter than the slowest chemical relaxation time (~600 s) yet long enough to minimise communication overhead.

5.2 Master Algorithm and Rollback Protocol

At each macro‑step k:

1. Discrete events (crew activity changes, equipment failure) that occur during (t_k, t_{k+1}) are collected. If any event changes a state variable (e.g., pump off), the affected FMU is flagged for update.
2. Each FMU receives input values (composition, power) for the next 300 s. Inputs are held constant (zero‑order hold) over the step.
3. Each FMU advances its internal state by calling doStep(300), returning at t_{k+1}.
4. Conservation audit: The master assembles full state vector \mathbf{x}^* from all FMUs. It checks:
   · Elemental mass closure using \|\mathbf{S}^T\mathbf{x}^* - \mathbf{c}\|. If > 10^{-6} kg, the step is rejected.
   · Total energy error; if > 10^{-3} kWh, rejected.
   · Computed entropy generation; if \dot{S}_{\text{gen}} < -10^{-3} W K⁻¹, rejected.
5. If any check fails, the master sends rollback commands to all FMUs (FMU‑provided get/setState) and requests a re‑do with macro‑step halved. After a maximum of three consecutive rejections, the simulation halts with an error.
6. If passed, the state is projected onto \mathcal{M} via the minimal‑norm projection (Theorem 1) and committed.

This protocol guarantees that no conservation violation survives even a single macro‑step.

5.3 Algebraic Loop Resolution

Where FMU outputs mutually depend (e.g., electrolyser H₂ feeds Sabatier, which determines CO₂ load for electrolyser scaling), an algebraic loop arises. The loop is broken by inserting a one‑macro‑step delay in the Sabatier’s H₂ demand, which is physically justified because the gas system has latency. The resulting error is a first‑order lag with time constant \Delta T_{\text{macro}}; analysis shows it contributes less than 0.1 % error in steady‑state composition.

5.4 Model Predictive Control for Resource Allocation

The master controller solves a finite‑horizon optimal control problem every hour (synchronised with the macro‑steps). The cost function:

J = \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} \bigl( \| \mathbf{x}_{t+n} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{ref}}\|_Q^2 + \| \mathbf{u}_{t+n} \|_R^2 \bigr) + \| \mathbf{x}_{t+N} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{ref}}\|_P^2,

subject to linearised dynamics \mathbf{x}_{t+n+1} = A\mathbf{x}_{t+n} + B\mathbf{u}_{t+n} + \mathbf{d}_{t+n}, with hard constraints on O₂ tank level (≥12 h reserve) and soft constraints on battery SOC (penalised quadratic). Horizon N is dynamically set to cover the longest time‑to‑criticality among resources, but capped at 48 steps (48 h). The QP is solved using a projected gradient method with warm start. The priority index from the original blueprint is replaced by the MPC cost weights; the oxygen weight is the highest.

5.5 Plugin Architecture

To enable community contributions, the simulator defines an abstract BasePlugin with hooks: onPreStep, onPostStep, onConservationCheck. Plugins must pass a dimensional‑consistency checker and a 100‑sol regression test before merging.

6. Validation Protocol

This chapter specifies the requirements that a future implementation must satisfy. No test results are presented.

6.1 Level 1 — Unit Validation

Every ODE right‑hand side must be compared against its source reference. For example, the Sabatier conversion vs. temperature curve must match Falbo et al. (2018) within 5 % at each octave of temperature. The RO water flux equation must reproduce manufacturer data within 5 %. Failure halts advancement.

6.2 Level 2 — Subsystem Validation

Using public ISS ECLSS logs from 2021‑2023, the water recovery subsystem must yield a long‑term mean WRR of 0.93\pm0.02 (Phase 1). The O2 system must match ISS OGS specific energy (54.2 kWh kg⁻¹) within 5 % and MOXIE flight data (6 g h⁻¹) within 20 %. Crew metabolism must lie within the 95 % confidence interval of HI‑SEAS V per‑person means. Comparison is performed using a two‑sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test at significance \alpha=0.05; the null hypothesis of identical distribution must not be rejected.

6.3 Level 3 — Integrated Scenario

A 90‑sol simulation with four crew, a global dust storm (\tau=4.5, sol 10–60) and a nuclear reactor failure (sol 25, 72‑h repair) must be run. The following must hold:

· No mass violation >10⁻⁶ kg at any coupling step.
· O₂ tank level never below 12 h reserve.
· Entropy generation never negative.
· The mean daily water recovery rate over the storm period must not differ from the nominal Phase 2 WRR by more than 5 % (t‑test).

6.4 Level 4 — Long‑Term and Uncertainty

A 1000‑sol run with random failure injection (Weibull, parameters as in Section 5) must pass the same conservation tests. The PCE‑based uncertainty analysis must produce a 95‑th percentile O₂ reserve curve that, at sol 200, is at least 5 % below the deterministic forecast (to demonstrate that uncertainty propagation works). The mean and variance of this curve must converge as the Smolyak grid level is increased; a Richardson extrapolation test will verify convergence.

6.5 External Benchmarks

All comparisons use publicly available data: ISS ECLSS (NASA OIIS portal), HI‑SEAS mission reports, MSL/RAD data (PDS), MOXIE telemetry (PDS), and KRUSTY test reports (OSTI). The specification requires that these datasets be downloaded and used exactly as provided, without modification.

7. Discussion

7.1 Epistemic Status
This dissertation is a formal specification. Its mathematical theorems are proved under stated assumptions. Its architectural claims (e.g., algebraic loop delay error estimate) are supported by engineering analysis but not by simulation. The projected real‑time performance (>1000:1) is a target derived from profiling analogous codes, not a measured result. All such projections must be verified during implementation.

7.2 Major Limitations
The metabolic gravity‑scaling remains an unvalidated extrapolation; the crop model is empirical Monod, not mechanistic; radiation transport is a simple exponential; and the FMI rollback mechanism assumes perfect get/set state fidelity—a demanding interface requirement. These are acknowledged as sources of model‑form uncertainty that will require future experimental campaigns to reduce.

7.3 The Value of Specification First
History shows that simulation tools developed without a formal conservation backbone often hide drift errors that surface only after long test runs. By proving the conservation property mathematically and designing the architecture around it, we ensure that any faithful implementation will inherit that guarantee. This transforms the simulation from a heuristic aid into an engineering‑grade decision tool for mission architecture.

8. Conclusion

This dissertation has delivered the complete mathematical foundation, process‑model derivations, co‑simulation architecture, and validation protocol for a Mars habitat life support simulator whose core conservation property is a provable theorem, not a hoped‑for outcome. No code has been written, no experiments performed, no models trained. What has been achieved is a blueprint that, when implemented according to the specification, will faithfully track C, H, O, and N atoms across years of simulated mission time, never violating the laws of thermodynamics, and will quantify its own uncertainties against peer‑reviewed external benchmarks. The path from planning to presence on Mars runs through simulators that cannot be wrong about physics; this framework ensures they will not be.

